Notice of the Supreme People's Court on Printing and Distributing the Provisions on the Scope of Cases Accepted by Maritime Courts

Fa (Jiao) Fa [1989] No. 6

To all local people's courts at various levels, military courts at various levels, railway transport intermediate courts and basic courts, and maritime courts:

The Provisions on the Scope of Cases Accepted by Maritime Courts are hereby printed and distributed to you for your earnest implementation.

May 13, 1989

Provisions on the Scope of Cases Accepted by Maritime Courts

In order to better exercise China's maritime judicial jurisdiction and give full play to the adjudicative function of maritime courts, in accordance with the General Principles of the Civil Law of the PRC, the Civil Procedure Law of the PRC (for Trial Implementation), and relevant international conventions to which China is a party, and with reference to international customary practices, and on the basis of summarizing the maritime adjudicative practice of China, the scope of cases accepted by maritime courts is hereby stipulated as follows:

Maritime courts shall accept the following cases between Chinese legal persons or citizens, between Chinese legal persons or citizens and foreign or regional legal persons or citizens, and between foreign or regional legal persons or citizens:

I. Cases of Maritime Tort Disputes

1. Cases of disputes over compensation for damages arising from collision of ships, including compensation for damages from indirect collisions such as wash damage;
2. Cases of disputes over compensation for damages arising from a ship striking structures and facilities on the sea, in navigable waters, or in ports, including cases of compensation for damages from a ship striking docks, breakwaters, piers, ship locks, bridges, as well as navigational aids and other maritime facilities;
3. Cases of disputes over compensation for damages caused by a ship to facilities installed in the air or laid on the seabed or underwater;
4. Cases of disputes over compensation for damages caused by a ship discharging or leaking hazardous substances or sewage, resulting in pollution of waters or damage to other ships or cargo;
5. Cases of disputes over compensation for damages from marine or port construction, operations, or shipbreaking causing pollution of waters or damage to other ships or cargo;
6. Cases of disputes over compensation for damages caused by a ship's navigation or operations damaging fishing nets, other fishing facilities, and aquaculture;
7. Cases of disputes over compensation for losses caused by sunken vessels, waste, or improper placement of marine operation facilities in navigation channels affecting the navigation of ships;
8. Cases of disputes over compensation for damages arising from personal injury or death in the course of maritime transport or operations at sea, in navigable waters, or in ports;
9. Cases of wrongful detention of ships or cargo on board;
10. Other cases of maritime tort disputes.

II. Cases of Maritime Contract Disputes

1. Cases of disputes over contracts for the carriage of goods by water, including cases of disputes over contracts for ocean carriage, international multimodal transport involving a sea leg, coastal and inland water transport, as well as water-water联运, water-land multimodal transport, and other contracts for the carriage of goods by water;
2. Cases of disputes over contracts for the carriage of passengers and their luggage by water;
3. Cases of disputes over contracts for the construction, sale, repair, and dismantling of ships;
4. Cases of disputes over loan contracts secured by a mortgage on a ship or by a pledge of the ship's operating income;
5. Cases of disputes over charter parties, including cases of disputes over bareboat charter parties, time charter parties, and voyage charter parties for seagoing vessels, as well as charter and lease contracts for coastal and inland water transport vessels;
6. Cases of disputes over ship agency contracts;
7. Cases of disputes over freight forwarding contracts;
8. Cases of disputes over contracts for the supply of goods necessary for the operation or daily needs of a ship;
9. Cases of disputes over seamen's employment contracts;
10. Cases of disputes over salvage and reclamation contracts;
11. Cases of disputes over towage contracts;
12. Cases of disputes over marine insurance contracts, including insurance contracts for the carriage of goods by sea, hull insurance, oil pollution and other protection and indemnity liability insurance, and personal accident insurance;
13. Cases of disputes over joint venture contracts for maritime transport;
14. Other cases of maritime contract disputes.

III. Other Maritime and Admiralty Cases

1. Cases of major liability accidents in maritime transport or marine operations (including fishing operations);
2. Cases of disputes over port operations, including cases of disputes over surveying, exploration, port construction, dredging, blasting, salvage, rescue, towing, above-water and underwater construction, port loading and unloading (loading and unloading, lightering, storage), and tallying operations within port areas;
3. Cases of disputes over general average;
4. Cases of disputes over compensation for loss of loading and unloading equipment, ship's gear, and containers;
5. Cases of disputes over the development and utilization of the ocean, including disputes over the development and utilization of the continental shelf (such as exploitation of offshore oil and natural gas), development and utilization of coastal zones (such as land reclamation, tidal flat utilization, mining, engineering construction), development and utilization of marine fisheries and aquaculture, seawater desalination and comprehensive utilization, marine underwater engineering, and marine scientific research;
6. Cases of disputes among co-owners of a ship over the operation, earnings, and distribution of the ship;
7. Cases of disputes over ownership, possession, mortgage, or maritime liens of ships;
8. Cases for the determination of derelict ships and other property at sea without an owner;
9. Administrative cases involving competent authorities of the ocean or inland waters;
10. Cases of maritime fraud;
11. Other cases that are provided by law to be accepted by maritime courts or are assigned by a higher people's court.

IV. Cases for Maritime Enforcement

1. Cases where the competent authority of the ocean or inland waters applies for compulsory enforcement according to law;
2. Cases where an award has been made by a maritime arbitration institution, and one party fails to perform the award within the prescribed time limit, and the other party applies for enforcement;
3. Cases where an application is made for enforcement of a document of debt confirmed by a notarial institution and related to a ship or ship operation;
4. Cases where, in accordance with the provisions of the Convention on the Recognition and Enforcement of Foreign Arbitral Awards adopted in New York in 1958, an application is made to a maritime court of China for recognition and enforcement of an arbitral award made by a foreign or regional arbitration institution;
5. Cases where, in accordance with a judicial assistance agreement concluded between China and a foreign country, or on the basis of the principle of reciprocity, assistance is requested in enforcing a judgment of a foreign court.

V. Cases for Preservation of Maritime Claims

1. Cases where a maritime claimant applies for the arrest of a ship before the commencement of proceedings in order to secure the maritime claim;
2. Cases where a maritime claimant, in accordance with the terms of a contract, applies for the seizure of cargo on board or ship's bunker fuel before the commencement of proceedings.

These Provisions shall come into force as of the date of promulgation. The provisions on the scope of cases accepted by maritime courts in the Decision of the Supreme People's Court on Several Issues concerning the Establishment of Maritime Courts dated November 28, 1984, shall be simultaneously abolished.
